%[text] **1. Stability** (25 marks)
%[text] **a) (5 marks) Is the open loop plant**
%[text] $G(s)=\\frac{s+3}{s^4 + 23 s^3 + 182 s^2 + 580 s + 600}$
%[text] **stable or unstable.**
clear 
syms s
RT=[s^4 1 182 600;s^3 23 580 0] %[output:12874507]
RT=[RT;s^2 -det([RT(1,2) RT(1,3);RT(2,2) RT(2,3)])/RT(2,2) -det([RT(1,2) RT(1,4);RT(2,2) RT(2,4)])/RT(2,2) 0] %[output:85af8804]
RT=[RT;s^1 -det([RT(2,2) RT(2,3);RT(3,2) RT(3,3)])/RT(3,2) -det([RT(2,2) RT(2,4);RT(3,2) RT(3,4)])/RT(3,2) 0] %[output:7e474624]
RT=[RT;s^0 -det([RT(3,2) RT(3,3);RT(4,2) RT(4,3)])/RT(4,2) -det([RT(3,2) RT(3,4);RT(4,2) RT(4,4)])/RT(4,2) 0] %[output:343d5a8c]

%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:374f)
%[text] **Consider the system shown above where:**
%[text] $D(s)=K\\;\\mbox{ and } \\;G(s)=\\frac{s+3}{s^4 + 23 s^3 + 182 s^2 + 580 s + 600}$
%[text] **b) (5 marks) Compute the closed loop transfer function of the system**
syms sigma s K 
% G=zpk([-3],[-2 -5 -6 -10],1)
G=tf([1 3], [1 23 182 580 600]) %[output:19e086c8]
[num,den]=tfdata(G) %[output:8ca7bb7f] %[output:80c843e7]
Gs=poly2sym(cell2mat(num),s)/poly2sym(cell2mat(den),s) %[output:1adc0268]
Gcls=K*Gs/(1+K*Gs) %[output:510cb221]
%[text] **c) (10 marks) For what values of K is the closed loop system stable?**
Gcls=collect(simplifyFraction(Gcls)) %[output:81830fcb]
RT=[s^4 1 182 3*K+600;s^3 23 K+580 0] %[output:208c8eec]
RT=[RT;s^2 -det([RT(1,2) RT(1,3);RT(2,2) RT(2,3)])/RT(2,2) -det([RT(1,2) RT(1,4);RT(2,2) RT(2,4)])/RT(2,2) 0] %[output:38645f30]
%[text] Multypliy $s^2$ row by 23.
RT(3,2)=23*RT(3,2) %[output:5a69fe12]
RT(3,3)=23*RT(3,3) %[output:6d5c0766]
%[text] Now continue.
RT=[RT;s^1 -det([RT(2,2) RT(2,3);RT(3,2) RT(3,3)])/RT(3,2) -det([RT(2,2) RT(2,4);RT(3,2) RT(3,4)])/RT(3,2) 0] %[output:2d142566]
RT=[RT;s^0 -det([RT(3,2) RT(3,3);RT(4,2) RT(4,3)])/RT(4,2) -det([RT(3,2) RT(3,4);RT(4,2) RT(4,4)])/RT(4,2) 0] %[output:273bf9d9]
RT(4,2)=simplifyFraction(RT(4,2)) %[output:2eddc07e]
%%
maybeK=roots([-1 1439 1774080]) %[output:471c91a4]
%[text] d) (5 marks) Calculate the $j\\omega$-axis crossings (frequency). From your answer to (1c) you know the value of $K$ where this occurs.
%[text] Two possible solutions:
%[text] Solution 1:
%[text] Take the solution of the polynomial corresponding to the $s^2$row of the Routh table.
roots([3606-maybeK(1) 0 69*maybeK(1)+13800]) %[output:38791b6b]
